{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3537","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3537","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3537\",\"parent_revision\":\"8f9b9750c25e5c3deb37350ffa6571ec94bd90434e7feaf286a6f1e1a0183dc1\",\"root_revision\":\"8f9b9750c25e5c3deb37350ffa6571ec94bd90434e7feaf286a6f1e1a0183dc1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-13\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n# Redesign of the research insight assessment step\\n\\nPlan of record. Sections 1-10 below are the requirement specification, not a summary of one.\\n\\n## Owner's stated intent\\n\\n> \\\"there was a dedicated agent that was used to do that step and that agent would read the research file that was created, and then read through the goals, missions, readmes, agents.md, architecture files in this repo and find domain overlaps where the local system could either borrow from the technique used by the research entry, or the research entry provided a tooling or system that could replace or improve an existing local process or system. and when it had a bunch of potential candidate matches it would then do a verification and deep dive into what and how - then create backlog items as required for the assessment and grooming of what could be done to use it or use its technique or methodology.\\\"\\n\\nPurpose the whole skill serves, owner's words:\\n\\n> \\\"creating single page references that assist with knowing the state of the art, and applying lessons from it to this repo\\\"\\n\\n## 0. Verified state of the current system\\n\\nEvery claim here was read, not recalled.\\n\\n- **The extractor's terminal stop is real.** `.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md:30-31` \u2014 flowchart node `Empty{}` routes to `Skip([\\\"Write: no actionable patterns found. Stop.\\\"])`. Restated as a rule at `:259`: \\\"If the entry has no Relevance or Patterns section, return `STATUS: no_actionable_patterns` and stop \u2014 do not write a file.\\\" The repo-search nodes at `:32-33` are downstream of that gate, so they only ever map what the entry's own section already named.\\n- **The assessor has the same shape, worse.** `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md:33` is a terminal early exit on the entry's own content. `:163-164` explicitly forbids reading local files on that path. Its discovery node `:35` is unguided prose (\\\"Identify local systems that could be callers\\\") with no index and no search procedure, on `model: haiku` (`:4`). It cannot file backlog items (`:156`).\\n- **Filing precedes verification.** `.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL.md:98-114` step 6a spawns the extractor, which files backlog items. `SKILL.md:122` step 8 runs Entry Review, whose Gate 4 (`references/entry-review-rubric.md:67-78`) is the path-exists / gap-is-real / signal-is-runnable check. On `REQUEST CHANGES`, `SKILL.md:302-306` withholds a README row and marks the entry \u2014 **nothing retracts the filed issue.** That ordering, not any agent's honesty, is what let the cocoindex item through.\\n- **Nothing mechanically checks analysis files.** `scripts/validate_research.py:731` excludes `insights`, `utilization`, `design-notes` from validation.\\n- **The Relevance section is a required section**, `scripts/validate_research.py:54`.\\n\\n### Two premises corrected by the owner; both were in the original brief and both are wrong\\n\\n1. **The twelve-row mapping table is not stale.** I resolved every path in `research-insight-extractor.md:57-68` \u2014 all exist. The two `implementation-manager` paths (`:59` in `development-harness`, `:144` in `python3-development`) are both real directories, not a contradiction. Auto-generating or refreshing the table would have prevented nothing.\\n2. **The 21-of-32 nonexistent paths are not extractor output.** They sit in entry \\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\" sections, written by `research-curator`, which has no mapping table and never read the repo. Separate population, separate cause. Not used as evidence about the extractor anywhere in this plan.\\n\\n**Related correction, and it changes the design's aim:** of roughly 99 broken repository citations across the corpus, exactly one was ever invented. `.claude/rules/` existed until PR #3391 renamed it to `rules/`; the swarm skills existed until PR #3422; `backlog-tools-administrator` until PR #3330. This is repo drift breaking correct citations, not agents inventing paths. Every evidence requirement below is aimed at **unsearched absence** and **obsolescence**, not at fabrication. Any reviewer who reads them as anti-hallucination machinery has the wrong model of the failure.\\n\\n### The table's actual defect: coverage\\n\\n12 rows. `research/` has **37 entry categories**. The single largest, `research/developer-tools/` (71 entries), maps to no row. Also unmapped: `ai-design-tools` (12), `data-infrastructure` (10), `ai-research-tools` (9), `api-frameworks` (8), `llm-infrastructure` (7), `prompt-engineering` (7), `ml-infrastructure` (6), `async-libraries` (4), `ai-writing-tools` (4), `documentation-tools` (3), `ai-observability` (3), plus ~14 singleton categories. Roughly half the corpus sits outside every row. An agent handed twelve rows stops at twelve rows. Not fixable by editing the table.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Where discovery happens\\n\\n**The extractor owns discovery. The Relevance section becomes the seed, not the source.**\\n\\nPR #3530's Repo Anchor Pass (`references/extraction-methodology.md` Phase 1c, branch `research-curator/relevance-anchors`) is correct and stays. But read what it says about itself: \\\"six Reads is its whole budget\u2026 Its output is what those agents start from instead of re-deriving the mapping.\\\" Six Reads against a 288-SKILL, 94-agent, 36-rule repo is a seed, and the PR author says so. A seed cannot be a source.\\n\\nThis adopts the PR author's **first** conditional answer (if discovery leaves the section, the section becomes findings whose job is to emit searchable domain terms), with one change: keep the anchors. They cost six Reads already spent, and a `grep \u2192 0 matches` record is a re-runnable term the extractor should not re-run.\\n\\nThe author's **second** conditional (discovery stays in the section, typed as candidates) is rejected for a mechanical reason: the entry is written by an agent that has just spent its context on the external resource, and the anchor budget is fixed at six Reads regardless of how many candidate domains the resource has. Put the expensive read where the context is free.\\n\\nConcretely:\\n\\n- Curator / anchor pass: unchanged from #3530. Keep.\\n- Extractor: delete the `Empty{}` gate (`:30-31`) and the `:259` stop rule. Discovery runs **unconditionally**, including on entries whose anchors are all zero-match. Those are precisely the entries where \\\"borrow the technique\\\" is the only available move, and the current design skips them outright.\\n- Extractor derives search terms from the entry's **mechanism sections** \u2014 `Problem Addressed`, `Key Features`, `Technical Architecture` \u2014 not only from Relevance. Terms already recorded in the Relevance section with a result are reused, not re-searched.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. What the agent reads, and how it searches\\n\\nThe owner named goals, missions, READMEs, `AGENTS.md`, architecture files. Measured, in this repo, those are:\\n\\n| Owner's word | This repo | Size | How the agent uses it |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| readmes | `README.md` root \u2014 the 29-plugin table, one-line domain per plugin (`README.md:60` onward) | 23.6 KB / ~6k tok | **Read in full, once, Phase A.** The only maintained, complete, domain-level index of the repo's capability surface. |\\n| agents.md | `AGENTS.md` (429 lines), `rules/AGENTS.md`, `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md` | 429 lines | **Grep, never read in full.** Read only the matched section. |\\n| goals | `SKILL-GOALS.md` \u2014 12 exist across `.claude/skills/*/` and `plugins/*/skills/*/` | ~8 lines each | Read when a candidate skill has one. Free, and it states what that skill is *for*. |\\n| missions / architecture | `rules/*.md` \u2014 **36 files, 1890 lines total, largest 142** | avg 52 lines | **The repo's actual policy corpus.** One topic per file, grep-addressable, ~700 tokens to read one. \\\"How this repo does X\\\" lives here, not in `docs/`. |\\n| \u2014 | `docs/*.md` (15 files) | \u2014 | Grep only. |\\n\\n**Explicitly excluded: `.claude/skills/README.md`.** Verified stale \u2014 it advertises `agent-creator`, `verify`, `rt-ica`, `subagent-contract`, `git-commit-helper`, `hooks-guide` as living in `.claude/skills/`; none are there. Any repo-reading agent that trusts it is misled. The extractor must not read it. (Separate item; out of scope here, but it is a live trap for every discovery agent.)\\n\\n**Search procedure \u2014 replaces the twelve-row table.** The already-working version of this is `.claude/skills/external-pattern-integrator/SKILL.md:86-107`: glob four patterns, match on the `description:` field only, priority High/Medium/Low. Reuse that procedure. Do not invent a second one.\\n\\n```\\nPhase A corpus, in order:\\n1. README.md                                            (~6k tok, read once)\\n2. description: lines from .claude/skills/*/SKILL.md    (17.5 KB / ~4.4k tok)\\n3. description: lines from .claude/agents/*.md          ( 6.9 KB / ~1.7k tok)\\n4. grep -ril \\\"{term}\\\" plugins/ .claude/ rules/ docs/ AGENTS.md   (3-6 terms)\\n5. For each plugin the above implicates (cap 3):\\n   description: lines from plugins/{p}/skills/*/SKILL.md and plugins/{p}/agents/*.md\\n   (development-harness is the worst case: 60 skills, 26.8 KB / ~6.7k tok)\\n```\\n\\nSteps 2, 3 and 5 are one `awk` over frontmatter. Nothing is read in full. Step 1 is what the twelve-row table was standing in for, and unlike the table it covers all 29 plugins including every domain the table never anticipated.\\n\\n**Why the table is deleted rather than extended:** `AGENTS.md:310-312` \u2014 \\\"Do not restate a value derived from a list, table, or directory defined elsewhere\u2026 Reference the source of truth instead.\\\" The table restates `README.md` plus a directory listing. It resolves today; it constrains the agent to twelve domains forever.\\n\\n### 2a. The 86% false-positive constraint, and this design's answer to it\\n\\n**Measured constraint that postdates the first draft of this plan:** an ungated path scan over an entry's \\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\" section measured roughly **86% false positives**, because entries describe the researched tool's own paths in the same paragraphs as this repo's. That is why closed PR #3527 had marker gating, and why its 40%/46% recall was the price of avoiding the 86%. **Removing gating without a different answer returns to 86%.**\\n\\nThis design's answer is **provenance, not markers**, and it survives the constraint by construction:\\n\\n- **No path in this pipeline ever originates in entry prose.** Paths enter only as the *output of a repo-side command*: `grep -ril` results, `ls`/glob results, or a `README.md` table row. The 86% false-positive population \u2014 the researched tool's own paths, sitting in the entry's paragraphs \u2014 is never a path source at any stage.\\n- A path that appears in an entry's prose is **the researched tool's path** until an independent repo-side search returns it. Appearing in both places is a coincidence to be established by the search, never assumed from the text.\\n- The Relevance section's #3530 anchors are admissible **because their provenance is repo-side**: Phase 1c produced them by grepping this repo, not by reading the resource. That is the distinction \u2014 not where a path is written, but which command produced it.\\n- Consequence for the extractor's instructions: the entry is read for **terms**, never for paths. Anyone implementing this who lets the extractor lift a path out of entry prose has reintroduced the 86%.\\n\\nThis is why no marker gating is needed and why the recall penalty PR #3527 paid does not apply: gating filtered a mixed population after the fact; provenance means the mixed population is never created.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. The two-phase shape\\n\\nBoth phases live in **one agent**, one invocation, with a hard artifact boundary between them.\\n\\n**Phase A \u2014 candidate matching. No full-file reads. Output is a list.**\\n\\nA *candidate* is a triple: `(local path, match verb, term that matched)`.\\n\\nTwo match verbs. This is where the utilization assessor's question survives \u2014 as a verb, not an agent:\\n\\n- `borrow` \u2014 the resource's *technique* could be applied to this local system.\\n- `depend` \u2014 the resource itself (API, package, CLI, MCP server) could be called by this local system.\\n\\nPhase A ends with the candidate list written into the improvements file **before** any deep read, including the zero-match records. A zero-candidate Phase A is a valid, complete result and still writes the file: the searched terms and their zero results are the finding. Same principle as Phase 1c's \\\"zero matches is an anchor\\\".\\n\\n**Phase B \u2014 verification and deep dive.**\\n\\nPromotion rule, explicit. A candidate promotes when **both**:\\n\\n1. Phase A's match was on a *mechanism* term, not the resource's brand name and not a generic word (`agent`, `tool`, `python`, `skill`); and\\n2. The candidate path is a `SKILL.md`, an agent definition, a `rules/*.md`, or a script \u2014 not a README, not an insights file, not a research entry.\\n\\nEverything else is recorded in the candidate table as `not promoted \u2014 {which rule}` and costs nothing further. Cap promotions at **5 per entry**. If more qualify, promote the 5 whose matched term appears most often across the entry's mechanism sections and **record the count that went unpromoted** \u2014 the same reporting discipline as Phase 1c step 4, which reports an incomplete pass rather than silently completing it.\\n\\nPhase B, per promoted candidate:\\n\\n1. Read the file. Read its `SKILL-GOALS.md` if one exists.\\n2. Record evidence in the form specified in \u00a73a below.\\n3. For a `depend` candidate, the integration surface is quoted from the **entry**, not recalled.\\n4. For any absence claim, record the search command and its result.\\n\\n### 3a. Evidence format \u2014 stored evidence must re-run or re-locate\\n\\n**Owner's ruling, and it overrides the first draft of this plan.** A `file:line` reference in a durable artifact is **stale by design**: any edit above the cited line silently invalidates it, and the artifact gives no signal that it happened. This is the same drift that broke ~99 correct citations (\u00a70).\\n\\nRequired form:\\n\\n- **Presence evidence**: the **path plus the exact quoted text**. Content-addressed and greppable \u2014 a reviewer re-locates it with `grep -F` regardless of how the file has moved underneath it.\\n- **Absence evidence**: the **exact search command and its result**, which is re-runnable. Unchanged from what #3530 already specifies.\\n- A line number **may** appear as a convenience. **Nothing may depend on it, and no check may treat it as authoritative.** A check that resolves a stored reference must use the quoted text; the line number is a hint for a human's first jump, nothing more.\\n\\n**#3530 is currently inconsistent on exactly this axis, verified on branch `research-curator/relevance-anchors`:**\\n\\n- Absence evidence is right \u2014 `research-insight-extractor.md:154` requires \\\"the exact search behind any 'no local system does X' claim, with its result\\\", which re-runs.\\n- Presence evidence is wrong \u2014 `:153` is `**Local system**: {path to the local file this maps to}`, a bare path with no quoted text, and `:112` says only \\\"Open every path you name before naming it\\\". A path alone neither re-runs nor re-locates; it just rots.\\n- The entry-template side of #3530 already models the right shape (`Today: \\\"{exact line, heading, table row, or config value read from that path}\\\"`). The extractor side did not inherit it.\\n\\nThis design must be consistent: **presence carries quoted text, absence carries the command, neither depends on a line number.** Implementing this closes the #3530 inconsistency rather than propagating it.\\n\\n### 3b. The filing gate \u2014 defer to #3535\\n\\n**No backlog item without a Phase B evidence record.** This is the same behaviour already specified in **#3535** (\\\"research-insight-extractor verifies proposal claims but nothing blocks filing a backlog item when verification fails\\\"). **#3535 is the specification. This item does not restate it, so the two cannot drift apart.** What this plan contributes is only the surrounding shape: the gate's evidence comes from Phase B, and its format is \u00a73a's.\\n\\nThe gate is what moves Gate 4's substance (`entry-review-rubric.md:71-76`) to *before* the write instead of after it.\\n\\nEntry Review's Gate 4 stays as the independent check \u2014 maker/checker is worth keeping \u2014 and gets one added line: **a Gate 4 defect naming a filed issue closes that issue** via `backlog_close`, with the defect text as the reason. One line, no machinery, and it closes the loop `SKILL.md:302-306` currently leaves open. Coordinate with **#3534**, which covers Gate 4's inability to express path drift; a Gate 4 that misattributes a repo refactor to the research agent would, with this retraction line added, close correct issues for the wrong reason. **#3534 must land before, or with, the retraction line.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Cost\\n\\nPrice basis: `claude-api` skill's model table, cached 2026-06-24 \u2014 Opus 5 $5/$25 per MTok, Sonnet 5 $2/$10, Haiku 4.5 $1/$5. **Token volumes are estimated from measured file sizes; the file sizes are measured, the volumes are not.**\\n\\nPer entry, new design:\\n\\n| | tokens |\\n|---|---|\\n| research entry (~15 KB avg across 348 entry files) | ~4k |\\n| Phase A corpus (README + local skill/agent descriptions) | ~12k |\\n| Phase A greps (3-6 terms) | ~2k |\\n| Phase A plugin drill-down (cap 3) | ~7k |\\n| Phase B reads (cap 5 files) | ~20k |\\n| **input** | **~45k** |\\n| improvements file + backlog bodies | **~4k out** |\\n\\n- **Sonnet 5**: 45k x $2/M + 4k x $10/M = **$0.13/entry** \u2192 **~$43** for a 331-entry backfill.\\n- Opus 5, same shape: $0.33/entry \u2192 ~$109.\\n- **Current design**, same estimation method: opus extractor ~25k in / 4k out = $0.23, plus haiku assessor ~15k in / 2k out = $0.025 \u2192 **~$0.25/entry, ~$83** \u2014 and it buys candidate matching against twelve hardcoded domains plus one file with no reader.\\n\\n**Ruling: Sonnet 5, effort `high`, one agent.**\\n\\n`rules/model-selection.md` says to split a checker from an analyzer. This deliberately does not: Phase A is haiku-shaped and Phase B is opus-shaped, but splitting doubles the entry read and adds a third agent to a per-entry fan-out that `SKILL-GOALS.md` goal 3 already caps at 5 concurrent. Sonnet is the honest single point. If measurement (\u00a76) shows proposal *quality* is the binding constraint, **raise the model \u2014 do not add a tier.**\\n\\n**One lever, named and deliberately not built:** the Phase A corpus is byte-identical across all 331 entries. In a subagent fan-out, prompt caching cannot span agents, so that ~12k is paid 331 times (~$8 at Sonnet input rates \u2014 not worth engineering around). A script-driven backfill through the Message Batches API would halve the whole bill. Scope note, not a requirement.\\n\\nWhat the money buys that today's does not: discovery reaching the ~158 entries in categories the twelve-row table has no row for, and issues that each carry a re-locatable quote.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. What gets deleted\\n\\n| Delete | Where | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` (whole agent, 164 lines) | \u2014 | Cannot file backlog items (`:156`); forbidden from reading local files on its early-exit path (`:163-164`); discovery node is unguided prose (`:35`); its 48 outputs have no reader. Its *question* survives as the `depend` match verb. |\\n| `## Mapping Patterns to Local Systems` | `research-insight-extractor.md:49-75` | 12 domains vs 37 categories; anchors the agent to its own rows. Replaced by \u00a72's procedure. Justified by `AGENTS.md:310-312`. **Not deleted for staleness \u2014 it resolves.** |\\n| `Empty{}` gate + `Skip` node | `research-insight-extractor.md:30-31` | Makes the entry's own section terminal. |\\n| `If the entry has no Relevance or Patterns section\u2026 stop` | `research-insight-extractor.md:259` | Same rule, second copy. |\\n| Utilization spawn + relay | `SKILL.md:106-108`, `:119`, `:153`, `:193`, `:198`, `:434` | Dead with the agent. Cuts per-entry fan-out from 3 to 2. |\\n| `research/insights/*-utilization.md` (48) and `research/utilization/*.md` (2) | \u2014 | **Only after \u00a76's baseline is measured, in a separate commit.** Per `AGENTS.md` \\\"Deletion safety\\\": no replacement carries their content forward, so this is a deliberate drop of superseded analysis, not a migration. Flag it to the owner as such before executing. |\\n\\n**Not deleted: the Relevance section.** Required at `validate_research.py:54`, and #3530 has just given it a shape that earns its keep as the extractor's seed. Deleting it is a 331-file migration that changes no outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. How anyone would know it worked\\n\\nRun the new extractor over the **20 entries that already have `-improvements.md` files** and compare, per entry:\\n\\n**Metric 1 \u2014 discovery independence. The one that distinguishes the two processes.**\\n> Share of proposals naming a local path that the entry's own Relevance section does not name.\\n\\nThe current extractor cannot exceed its Relevance section by construction (`:30-33`), so its baseline is near zero by design, and the measurement confirms or refutes that reading. The new process should be high. This is the single number separating \\\"found an overlap\\\" from \\\"inherited a claim\\\".\\n\\n**Metric 2 \u2014 category reach.**\\n> Proposals per entry, grouped by `research/` category, old vs new.\\n\\nSpecific and falsifiable: the gain should concentrate in the ~158 entries in categories with no table row (`developer-tools`, `ai-design-tools`, `data-infrastructure`, \u2026) and be near zero in the eight categories the table already covered. **If the gain is flat across categories, the table was not the constraint and this plan misdiagnosed the problem.**\\n\\n**Metric 3 \u2014 unsearched absence.**\\n> For every proposal claiming \\\"no local system does X\\\": does the recorded search command, re-run today, still return zero?\\n\\nThe cocoindex item is a metric-3 failure \u2014 `research/insights/2026-03-10-cocoindex-code-improvements.md:37` asserts no semantic code search exists while `plugins/python3-development/skills/semantic-code-search/SKILL.md` and `plugins/python-engineering/agents/semantic-code-search.md` both do. One `grep -ril \\\"semantic\\\" plugins/` would have caught it. Automatable; should be 100% and stay 100%.\\n\\n**Metric 4 \u2014 yield per dollar.**\\n> Items filed per entry x cost per entry, and \u2014 30 days on \u2014 how many of those issues are open, closed-as-done, or closed-as-wrong.\\n\\nThe only metric testing the owner's actual constraint. Metrics 1-3 can all improve while metric 4 says the step should run on a subset.\\n\\n**Baseline to capture before deleting anything:** metrics 1 and 3 over the existing 123 improvements files and 48 utilization files. That is the only remaining reason those 48 files exist.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. The heading\\n\\n`## Relevance to Claude Code Development` names Claude Code the product where the content must describe **this repository**. An agent writing generically about Claude Code skills has obeyed the heading exactly.\\n\\n**This design does not need the rename.** The extractor greps the section for terms; the heading text is not load-bearing for any reader this plan adds. #3530's subject-defining note in `entry-template.md` binds the *writer*, which is where the ambiguity actually bites.\\n\\n**But the rename's cost has been overstated. It is not 331 entries.** `validate_research.py:63` already carries `SECTION_ALIASES`, resolved at `:237-238` as canonical \u2192 accepted alternates. A rename is:\\n\\n1. `REQUIRED_BODY_SECTIONS` (`:54`): swap the string.\\n2. `SECTION_ALIASES` (`:63`): add `\\\"{new}\\\": [\\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\"]`.\\n3. `entry-review-rubric.md:69` and `:84`: update the two mentions.\\n4. `entry-template.md`: new heading for new entries.\\n\\nExisting entries keep validating via the alias. **Two lines of Python, two lines of prose, zero entry edits.**\\n\\nRecommendation: **don't, now** \u2014 it changes no outcome and the note already covers the writer. If the owner wants it anyway, it is cheap, and the name should state subject and job rather than a product: **`## Repo Anchors`** (matching the Phase 1c artifact it is written from), or `## This Repository \u2014 Anchors and Overlaps` if the shorter name reads too terse in a table of contents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Execution order\\n\\nSequenced against what is in flight. Nothing here touches `entry-template.md`, `extraction-methodology.md`, or `.claude/agents/research-curator.md` \u2014 those belong to the entry-production agent.\\n\\n1. **Wait for `research-curator/relevance-anchors` (#3530) to land.** Phase 1c is this design's seed; editing the extractor against a moving Relevance shape is rework.\\n2. **Wait for `research-curator/cull` (#3529) to land.** It touches `SKILL.md`, so \u00a75's `SKILL.md` deletions conflict textually otherwise. It deletes `.claude/agents/research-context-agent.md` \u2014 whose Phase 2 \\\"Search & Match\\\" is the closest existing relative of what \u00a72 specifies. **Read it before writing \u00a72's procedure and take its dimension table's shape, not its terminal action:** it appended prose to the research entry, which is why nothing ever consumed it. This design's terminal action is a backlog item, which has a consumer.\\n3. **Baseline measurement** (\u00a76 metrics 1 and 3 over the existing 123 + 48 analysis files). Before any deletion.\\n4. **Edit `research-insight-extractor.md`:** delete `:30-31`, `:49-75`, `:259`; rewrite the flowchart as Phase A / Phase B; add the `borrow`/`depend` verbs, the promotion rule, the cap-5, \u00a72a's provenance rule, \u00a73a's evidence format; set `model: sonnet` in frontmatter (currently `opus` at `:4`). Keep #3530's `**Absence evidence**` field \u2014 it becomes the filing gate's absence slot rather than an advisory \u2014 and fix `:153` to carry quoted text per \u00a73a.\\n5. **Delete `research-utilization-assessor.md`; strip its spawn and relay from `SKILL.md`** (`:106-108`, `:119`, `:153`, `:193`, `:198`, `:434`).\\n6. **Add the Gate 4 retraction line** to `SKILL.md`'s Entry Review section (`:300-306`) \u2014 **after or with #3534.**\\n7. **A/B on 20 entries**; report metrics 1-4.\\n8. **Delete the 50 utilization files**, separate commit, only after step 7.\\n\\nSteps 4-6 are one PR. Steps 3, 7, 8 are separate. The filing gate itself is #3535's work, not this item's.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Goal tension against the frozen SKILL-GOALS.md\\n\\n`.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL-GOALS.md`'s six goals are APPROVED and FROZEN. No edit to any goal is proposed. One tension to report:\\n\\n**Goal 5** \u2014 \\\"automatically extract improvement proposals into the backlog, **assess direct utilization/integration opportunities**, and build a bidirectional cross-reference graph, so a new entry does not sit inert.\\\"\\n\\nDeleting the utilization assessor does **not** make goal 5 unreachable: the utilization question survives as the `depend` match verb and, for the first time, can produce a backlog item \u2014 which the assessor was forbidden from doing (`:156`). The capability is strengthened.\\n\\nThe tension is narrower, and is flagged rather than resolved: **if the owner reads \\\"assess\\\" as requiring a distinct artifact per entry**, then deleting `*-utilization.md` conflicts with that reading. The position taken here is that goal 5 names a capability, not an agent and not a file, and that a file whose only consumer is a count relayed to the user once is exactly what the standing \\\"remove everything that doesn't change the outcome\\\" mandate targets. **The owner should confirm that reading before step 8 executes.**\\n\\nGoals 1, 2, 4, 6 unaffected. Goal 3 (parallel batch, up to 5 concurrent) is helped: per-entry fan-out drops from 3 agents to 2.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Uncertainties, and what would resolve each\\n\\n| Uncertain | Resolution |\\n|---|---|\\n| How often the `Empty{}` stop actually fires. 123 improvements files exist for 331 entries, but that gap cannot be attributed to the stop node versus incomplete runs. | One grep: count entries whose Relevance section has no list items. Part of \u00a76's baseline. Does not change the plan \u2014 the node goes either way \u2014 but it sizes the gain. |\\n| Token estimates in \u00a74 are derived from measured file sizes, not from a run. | Instrument the 20-entry A/B (step 7) and report actual `usage` per entry. |\\n| Whether Sonnet 5 holds proposal quality at Phase B. | Step 7: run 5 of the 20 on Opus 5 and compare Gate 4 defect rates. If Sonnet's defect rate is materially higher, raise the model \u2014 the delta across a full backfill is ~$66, inside the noise of the decision. |\\n| Whether \u00a72a's provenance rule actually holds the 86% down in practice. **This is the plan's largest open risk.** The argument is structural, not measured. | Step 7 measures it directly: for every path in every new proposal, confirm the repo-side command that produced it. Any path traceable only to entry prose is a provenance breach and the rule needs strengthening, or gating after all. |\\n| Whether `ccc` (semantic search) would beat grep for Phase A. `.cocoindex_code/` exists at the repo root but `ccc` is **not installed** in this environment (`command not found`). | Do not design for it. If it becomes reliably present, it substitutes for \u00a72 step 4 with no other change. |\\n| Whether the cap-5 Phase B promotion limit is right. | Step 7 reports, per entry, how many candidates qualified and how many were cut. If entries routinely cut more than 5, raise it; if they rarely reach 5, the cap is free. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Critical files\\n\\n- `.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md`\\n- `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/research-curator/references/entry-review-rubric.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/external-pattern-integrator/SKILL.md` (lines 86-107 \u2014 the candidate-scan procedure to reuse, not reinvent)\\n\\n## Related items\\n\\n- **#3535** \u2014 the filing gate. **Same behaviour as this plan's \u00a73b; #3535 is the specification and this item defers to it.**\\n- **#3534** \u2014 Gate 4 cannot express path drift, so it attributes repo refactors to research agents. Must land before or with \u00a78 step 6's retraction line.\\n- **#3536** \u2014 Rule 2a enforced retroactively.\\n- PR #3529 (`research-curator/cull`) and PR #3530 (`research-curator/relevance-anchors`) \u2014 both must land first, per \u00a78.\\n- PRs #3527 and #3528 are being closed unmerged. **Do not build on either.** #3527's marker gating is referenced in \u00a72a only as the measured evidence for the 86% constraint, not as a design to adopt.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Planning session, 2026-09-13. Repo owner's stated design intent for the research-curator analysis fan-out. Related: #3534, #3535, #3536. In flight: PR #3529 (branch research-curator/cull), PR #3530 (branch research-curator/relevance-anchors). Closed unmerged, do not build on: #3527, #3528.\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-13\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## 3 The Two Phase Shape\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nBoth phases live in **one agent**, one invocation, with a hard artifact boundary between them.\\n\\n**Phase A \u2014 candidate matching. No full-file reads. Output is a list.**\\n\\nA *candidate* is a triple: `(local path, match verb, term that matched)`.\\n\\nTwo match verbs. This is where the utilization assessor's question survives \u2014 as a verb, not an agent:\\n\\n- `borrow` \u2014 the resource's *technique* could be applied to this local system.\\n- `depend` \u2014 the resource itself (API, package, CLI, MCP server) could be called by this local system.\\n\\nPhase A ends with the candidate list written into the improvements file **before** any deep read, including the zero-match records. A zero-candidate Phase A is a valid, complete result and still writes the file: the searched terms and their zero results are the finding. Same principle as Phase 1c's \\\"zero matches is an anchor\\\".\\n\\n**Phase B \u2014 verification and deep dive.**\\n\\nPromotion rule, explicit. A candidate promotes when **both**:\\n\\n1. Phase A's match was on a *mechanism* term, not the resource's brand name and not a generic word (`agent`, `tool`, `python`, `skill`); and\\n2. The candidate path is a `SKILL.md`, an agent definition, a `rules/*.md`, or a script \u2014 not a README, not an insights file, not a research entry.\\n\\nEverything else is recorded in the candidate table as `not promoted \u2014 {which rule}` and costs nothing further. Cap promotions at **5 per entry**. If more qualify, promote the 5 whose matched term appears most often across the entry's mechanism sections and **record the count that went unpromoted** \u2014 the same reporting discipline as Phase 1c step 4, which reports an incomplete pass rather than silently completing it.\\n\\nPhase B, per promoted candidate:\\n\\n1. Read the file. Read its `SKILL-GOALS.md` if one exists.\\n2. Record evidence in the form specified in \u00a73a below.\\n3. For a `depend` candidate, the integration surface is quoted from the **entry**, not recalled.\\n4. For any absence claim, record the search command and its result.\\n\\n### 3a. Evidence format \u2014 stored evidence must re-run or re-locate\\n\\n**Owner's ruling, and it overrides the first draft of this plan.** A `file:line` reference in a durable artifact is **stale by design**: any edit above the cited line silently invalidates it, and the artifact gives no signal that it happened. This is the same drift that broke ~99 correct citations (\u00a70).\\n\\nRequired form:\\n\\n- **Presence evidence**: the **path plus the exact quoted text**. Content-addressed and greppable \u2014 a reviewer re-locates it with `grep -F` regardless of how the file has moved underneath it.\\n- **Absence evidence**: the **exact search command and its result**, which is re-runnable. Unchanged from what #3530 already specifies.\\n- A line number **may** appear as a convenience. **Nothing may depend on it, and no check may treat it as authoritative.** A check that resolves a stored reference must use the quoted text; the line number is a hint for a human's first jump, nothing more.\\n\\n**#3530 is currently inconsistent on exactly this axis, verified on branch `research-curator/relevance-anchors`:**\\n\\n- Absence evidence is right \u2014 `research-insight-extractor.md:154` requires \\\"the exact search behind any 'no local system does X' claim, with its result\\\", which re-runs.\\n- Presence evidence is wrong \u2014 `:153` is `**Local system**: {path to the local file this maps to}`, a bare path with no quoted text, and `:112` says only \\\"Open every path you name before naming it\\\". A path alone neither re-runs nor re-locates; it just rots.\\n- The entry-template side of #3530 already models the right shape (`Today: \\\"{exact line, heading, table row, or config value read from that path}\\\"`). The extractor side did not inherit it.\\n\\nThis design must be consistent: **presence carries quoted text, absence carries the command, neither depends on a line number.** Implementing this closes the #3530 inconsistency rather than propagating it.\\n\\n### 3b. The filing gate \u2014 defer to #3535\\n\\n**No backlog item without a Phase B evidence record.** This is the same behaviour already specified in **#3535** (\\\"research-insight-extractor verifies proposal claims but nothing blocks filing a backlog item when verification fails\\\"). **#3535 is the specification. This item does not restate it, so the two cannot drift apart.** What this plan contributes is only the surrounding shape: the gate's evidence comes from Phase B, and its format is \u00a73a's.\\n\\nThe gate is what moves Gate 4's substance (`entry-review-rubric.md:71-76`) to *before* the write instead of after it.\\n\\nEntry Review's Gate 4 stays as the independent check \u2014 maker/checker is worth keeping \u2014 and gets one added line: **a Gate 4 defect naming a filed issue closes that issue** via `backlog_close`, with the defect text as the reason. One line, no machinery, and it closes the loop `SKILL.md:302-306` currently leaves open. Coordinate with **#3534**, which covers Gate 4's inability to express path drift; a Gate 4 that misattributes a repo refactor to the research agent would, with this retraction line added, close correct issues for the wrong reason. **#3534 must land before, or with, the retraction line.**\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 9 Goal Tension Against The Frozen Skill Goals Md\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL-GOALS.md`'s six goals are APPROVED and FROZEN. No edit to any goal is proposed. One tension to report:\\n\\n**Goal 5** \u2014 \\\"automatically extract improvement proposals into the backlog, **assess direct utilization/integration opportunities**, and build a bidirectional cross-reference graph, so a new entry does not sit inert.\\\"\\n\\nDeleting the utilization assessor does **not** make goal 5 unreachable: the utilization question survives as the `depend` match verb and, for the first time, can produce a backlog item \u2014 which the assessor was forbidden from doing (`:156`). The capability is strengthened.\\n\\nThe tension is narrower, and is flagged rather than resolved: **if the owner reads \\\"assess\\\" as requiring a distinct artifact per entry**, then deleting `*-utilization.md` conflicts with that reading. The position taken here is that goal 5 names a capability, not an agent and not a file, and that a file whose only consumer is a count relayed to the user once is exactly what the standing \\\"remove everything that doesn't change the outcome\\\" mandate targets. **The owner should confirm that reading before step 8 executes.**\\n\\nGoals 1, 2, 4, 6 unaffected. Goal 3 (parallel batch, up to 5 concurrent) is helped: per-entry fan-out drops from 3 agents to 2.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 2 What The Agent Reads And How It Searches\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe owner named goals, missions, READMEs, `AGENTS.md`, architecture files. Measured, in this repo, those are:\\n\\n| Owner's word | This repo | Size | How the agent uses it |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| readmes | `README.md` root \u2014 the 29-plugin table, one-line domain per plugin (`README.md:60` onward) | 23.6 KB / ~6k tok | **Read in full, once, Phase A.** The only maintained, complete, domain-level index of the repo's capability surface. |\\n| agents.md | `AGENTS.md` (429 lines), `rules/AGENTS.md`, `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md` | 429 lines | **Grep, never read in full.** Read only the matched section. |\\n| goals | `SKILL-GOALS.md` \u2014 12 exist across `.claude/skills/*/` and `plugins/*/skills/*/` | ~8 lines each | Read when a candidate skill has one. Free, and it states what that skill is *for*. |\\n| missions / architecture | `rules/*.md` \u2014 **36 files, 1890 lines total, largest 142** | avg 52 lines | **The repo's actual policy corpus.** One topic per file, grep-addressable, ~700 tokens to read one. \\\"How this repo does X\\\" lives here, not in `docs/`. |\\n| \u2014 | `docs/*.md` (15 files) | \u2014 | Grep only. |\\n\\n**Explicitly excluded: `.claude/skills/README.md`.** Verified stale \u2014 it advertises `agent-creator`, `verify`, `rt-ica`, `subagent-contract`, `git-commit-helper`, `hooks-guide` as living in `.claude/skills/`; none are there. Any repo-reading agent that trusts it is misled. The extractor must not read it. (Separate item; out of scope here, but it is a live trap for every discovery agent.)\\n\\n**Search procedure \u2014 replaces the twelve-row table.** The already-working version of this is `.claude/skills/external-pattern-integrator/SKILL.md:86-107`: glob four patterns, match on the `description:` field only, priority High/Medium/Low. Reuse that procedure. Do not invent a second one.\\n\\n```\\nPhase A corpus, in order:\\n1. README.md                                            (~6k tok, read once)\\n2. description: lines from .claude/skills/*/SKILL.md    (17.5 KB / ~4.4k tok)\\n3. description: lines from .claude/agents/*.md          ( 6.9 KB / ~1.7k tok)\\n4. grep -ril \\\"{term}\\\" plugins/ .claude/ rules/ docs/ AGENTS.md   (3-6 terms)\\n5. For each plugin the above implicates (cap 3):\\n   description: lines from plugins/{p}/skills/*/SKILL.md and plugins/{p}/agents/*.md\\n   (development-harness is the worst case: 60 skills, 26.8 KB / ~6.7k tok)\\n```\\n\\nSteps 2, 3 and 5 are one `awk` over frontmatter. Nothing is read in full. Step 1 is what the twelve-row table was standing in for, and unlike the table it covers all 29 plugins including every domain the table never anticipated.\\n\\n**Why the table is deleted rather than extended:** `AGENTS.md:310-312` \u2014 \\\"Do not restate a value derived from a list, table, or directory defined elsewhere\u2026 Reference the source of truth instead.\\\" The table restates `README.md` plus a directory listing. It resolves today; it constrains the agent to twelve domains forever.\\n\\n### 2a. The 86% false-positive constraint, and this design's answer to it\\n\\n**Measured constraint that postdates the first draft of this plan:** an ungated path scan over an entry's \\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\" section measured roughly **86% false positives**, because entries describe the researched tool's own paths in the same paragraphs as this repo's. That is why closed PR #3527 had marker gating, and why its 40%/46% recall was the price of avoiding the 86%. **Removing gating without a different answer returns to 86%.**\\n\\nThis design's answer is **provenance, not markers**, and it survives the constraint by construction:\\n\\n- **No path in this pipeline ever originates in entry prose.** Paths enter only as the *output of a repo-side command*: `grep -ril` results, `ls`/glob results, or a `README.md` table row. The 86% false-positive population \u2014 the researched tool's own paths, sitting in the entry's paragraphs \u2014 is never a path source at any stage.\\n- A path that appears in an entry's prose is **the researched tool's path** until an independent repo-side search returns it. Appearing in both places is a coincidence to be established by the search, never assumed from the text.\\n- The Relevance section's #3530 anchors are admissible **because their provenance is repo-side**: Phase 1c produced them by grepping this repo, not by reading the resource. That is the distinction \u2014 not where a path is written, but which command produced it.\\n- Consequence for the extractor's instructions: the entry is read for **terms**, never for paths. Anyone implementing this who lets the extractor lift a path out of entry prose has reintroduced the 86%.\\n\\nThis is why no marker gating is needed and why the recall penalty PR #3527 paid does not apply: gating filtered a mixed population after the fact; provenance means the mixed population is never created.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 4 Cost\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPrice basis: `claude-api` skill's model table, cached 2026-06-24 \u2014 Opus 5 $5/$25 per MTok, Sonnet 5 $2/$10, Haiku 4.5 $1/$5. **Token volumes are estimated from measured file sizes; the file sizes are measured, the volumes are not.**\\n\\nPer entry, new design:\\n\\n| | tokens |\\n|---|---|\\n| research entry (~15 KB avg across 348 entry files) | ~4k |\\n| Phase A corpus (README + local skill/agent descriptions) | ~12k |\\n| Phase A greps (3-6 terms) | ~2k |\\n| Phase A plugin drill-down (cap 3) | ~7k |\\n| Phase B reads (cap 5 files) | ~20k |\\n| **input** | **~45k** |\\n| improvements file + backlog bodies | **~4k out** |\\n\\n- **Sonnet 5**: 45k x $2/M + 4k x $10/M = **$0.13/entry** \u2192 **~$43** for a 331-entry backfill.\\n- Opus 5, same shape: $0.33/entry \u2192 ~$109.\\n- **Current design**, same estimation method: opus extractor ~25k in / 4k out = $0.23, plus haiku assessor ~15k in / 2k out = $0.025 \u2192 **~$0.25/entry, ~$83** \u2014 and it buys candidate matching against twelve hardcoded domains plus one file with no reader.\\n\\n**Ruling: Sonnet 5, effort `high`, one agent.**\\n\\n`rules/model-selection.md` says to split a checker from an analyzer. This deliberately does not: Phase A is haiku-shaped and Phase B is opus-shaped, but splitting doubles the entry read and adds a third agent to a per-entry fan-out that `SKILL-GOALS.md` goal 3 already caps at 5 concurrent. Sonnet is the honest single point. If measurement (\u00a76) shows proposal *quality* is the binding constraint, **raise the model \u2014 do not add a tier.**\\n\\n**One lever, named and deliberately not built:** the Phase A corpus is byte-identical across all 331 entries. In a subagent fan-out, prompt caching cannot span agents, so that ~12k is paid 331 times (~$8 at Sonnet input rates \u2014 not worth engineering around). A script-driven backfill through the Message Batches API would halve the whole bill. Scope note, not a requirement.\\n\\nWhat the money buys that today's does not: discovery reaching the ~158 entries in categories the twelve-row table has no row for, and issues that each carry a re-locatable quote.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Owner S Stated Intent\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n> \\\"there was a dedicated agent that was used to do that step and that agent would read the research file that was created, and then read through the goals, missions, readmes, agents.md, architecture files in this repo and find domain overlaps where the local system could either borrow from the technique used by the research entry, or the research entry provided a tooling or system that could replace or improve an existing local process or system. and when it had a bunch of potential candidate matches it would then do a verification and deep dive into what and how - then create backlog items as required for the assessment and grooming of what could be done to use it or use its technique or methodology.\\\"\\n\\nPurpose the whole skill serves, owner's words:\\n\\n> \\\"creating single page references that assist with knowing the state of the art, and applying lessons from it to this repo\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## 7 The Heading\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`## Relevance to Claude Code Development` names Claude Code the product where the content must describe **this repository**. An agent writing generically about Claude Code skills has obeyed the heading exactly.\\n\\n**This design does not need the rename.** The extractor greps the section for terms; the heading text is not load-bearing for any reader this plan adds. #3530's subject-defining note in `entry-template.md` binds the *writer*, which is where the ambiguity actually bites.\\n\\n**But the rename's cost has been overstated. It is not 331 entries.** `validate_research.py:63` already carries `SECTION_ALIASES`, resolved at `:237-238` as canonical \u2192 accepted alternates. A rename is:\\n\\n1. `REQUIRED_BODY_SECTIONS` (`:54`): swap the string.\\n2. `SECTION_ALIASES` (`:63`): add `\\\"{new}\\\": [\\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\"]`.\\n3. `entry-review-rubric.md:69` and `:84`: update the two mentions.\\n4. `entry-template.md`: new heading for new entries.\\n\\nExisting entries keep validating via the alias. **Two lines of Python, two lines of prose, zero entry edits.**\\n\\nRecommendation: **don't, now** \u2014 it changes no outcome and the note already covers the writer. If the owner wants it anyway, it is cheap, and the name should state subject and job rather than a product: **`## Repo Anchors`** (matching the Phase 1c artifact it is written from), or `## This Repository \u2014 Anchors and Overlaps` if the shorter name reads too terse in a table of contents.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 10 Uncertainties And What Would Resolve Each\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Uncertain | Resolution |\\n|---|---|\\n| How often the `Empty{}` stop actually fires. 123 improvements files exist for 331 entries, but that gap cannot be attributed to the stop node versus incomplete runs. | One grep: count entries whose Relevance section has no list items. Part of \u00a76's baseline. Does not change the plan \u2014 the node goes either way \u2014 but it sizes the gain. |\\n| Token estimates in \u00a74 are derived from measured file sizes, not from a run. | Instrument the 20-entry A/B (step 7) and report actual `usage` per entry. |\\n| Whether Sonnet 5 holds proposal quality at Phase B. | Step 7: run 5 of the 20 on Opus 5 and compare Gate 4 defect rates. If Sonnet's defect rate is materially higher, raise the model \u2014 the delta across a full backfill is ~$66, inside the noise of the decision. |\\n| Whether \u00a72a's provenance rule actually holds the 86% down in practice. **This is the plan's largest open risk.** The argument is structural, not measured. | Step 7 measures it directly: for every path in every new proposal, confirm the repo-side command that produced it. Any path traceable only to entry prose is a provenance breach and the rule needs strengthening, or gating after all. |\\n| Whether `ccc` (semantic search) would beat grep for Phase A. `.cocoindex_code/` exists at the repo root but `ccc` is **not installed** in this environment (`command not found`). | Do not design for it. If it becomes reliably present, it substitutes for \u00a72 step 4 with no other change. |\\n| Whether the cap-5 Phase B promotion limit is right. | Step 7 reports, per entry, how many candidates qualified and how many were cut. If entries routinely cut more than 5, raise it; if they rarely reach 5, the cap is free. |\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Critical Files\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md`\\n- `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/research-curator/references/entry-review-rubric.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/external-pattern-integrator/SKILL.md` (lines 86-107 \u2014 the candidate-scan procedure to reuse, not reinvent)\\n</div>\\n\\n## 1 Where Discovery Happens\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**The extractor owns discovery. The Relevance section becomes the seed, not the source.**\\n\\nPR #3530's Repo Anchor Pass (`references/extraction-methodology.md` Phase 1c, branch `research-curator/relevance-anchors`) is correct and stays. But read what it says about itself: \\\"six Reads is its whole budget\u2026 Its output is what those agents start from instead of re-deriving the mapping.\\\" Six Reads against a 288-SKILL, 94-agent, 36-rule repo is a seed, and the PR author says so. A seed cannot be a source.\\n\\nThis adopts the PR author's **first** conditional answer (if discovery leaves the section, the section becomes findings whose job is to emit searchable domain terms), with one change: keep the anchors. They cost six Reads already spent, and a `grep \u2192 0 matches` record is a re-runnable term the extractor should not re-run.\\n\\nThe author's **second** conditional (discovery stays in the section, typed as candidates) is rejected for a mechanical reason: the entry is written by an agent that has just spent its context on the external resource, and the anchor budget is fixed at six Reads regardless of how many candidate domains the resource has. Put the expensive read where the context is free.\\n\\nConcretely:\\n\\n- Curator / anchor pass: unchanged from #3530. Keep.\\n- Extractor: delete the `Empty{}` gate (`:30-31`) and the `:259` stop rule. Discovery runs **unconditionally**, including on entries whose anchors are all zero-match. Those are precisely the entries where \\\"borrow the technique\\\" is the only available move, and the current design skips them outright.\\n- Extractor derives search terms from the entry's **mechanism sections** \u2014 `Problem Addressed`, `Key Features`, `Technical Architecture` \u2014 not only from Relevance. Terms already recorded in the Relevance section with a result are reused, not re-searched.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 0 Verified State Of The Current System\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEvery claim here was read, not recalled.\\n\\n- **The extractor's terminal stop is real.** `.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md:30-31` \u2014 flowchart node `Empty{}` routes to `Skip([\\\"Write: no actionable patterns found. Stop.\\\"])`. Restated as a rule at `:259`: \\\"If the entry has no Relevance or Patterns section, return `STATUS: no_actionable_patterns` and stop \u2014 do not write a file.\\\" The repo-search nodes at `:32-33` are downstream of that gate, so they only ever map what the entry's own section already named.\\n- **The assessor has the same shape, worse.** `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md:33` is a terminal early exit on the entry's own content. `:163-164` explicitly forbids reading local files on that path. Its discovery node `:35` is unguided prose (\\\"Identify local systems that could be callers\\\") with no index and no search procedure, on `model: haiku` (`:4`). It cannot file backlog items (`:156`).\\n- **Filing precedes verification.** `.claude/skills/research-curator/SKILL.md:98-114` step 6a spawns the extractor, which files backlog items. `SKILL.md:122` step 8 runs Entry Review, whose Gate 4 (`references/entry-review-rubric.md:67-78`) is the path-exists / gap-is-real / signal-is-runnable check. On `REQUEST CHANGES`, `SKILL.md:302-306` withholds a README row and marks the entry \u2014 **nothing retracts the filed issue.** That ordering, not any agent's honesty, is what let the cocoindex item through.\\n- **Nothing mechanically checks analysis files.** `scripts/validate_research.py:731` excludes `insights`, `utilization`, `design-notes` from validation.\\n- **The Relevance section is a required section**, `scripts/validate_research.py:54`.\\n\\n### Two premises corrected by the owner; both were in the original brief and both are wrong\\n\\n1. **The twelve-row mapping table is not stale.** I resolved every path in `research-insight-extractor.md:57-68` \u2014 all exist. The two `implementation-manager` paths (`:59` in `development-harness`, `:144` in `python3-development`) are both real directories, not a contradiction. Auto-generating or refreshing the table would have prevented nothing.\\n2. **The 21-of-32 nonexistent paths are not extractor output.** They sit in entry \\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\" sections, written by `research-curator`, which has no mapping table and never read the repo. Separate population, separate cause. Not used as evidence about the extractor anywhere in this plan.\\n\\n**Related correction, and it changes the design's aim:** of roughly 99 broken repository citations across the corpus, exactly one was ever invented. `.claude/rules/` existed until PR #3391 renamed it to `rules/`; the swarm skills existed until PR #3422; `backlog-tools-administrator` until PR #3330. This is repo drift breaking correct citations, not agents inventing paths. Every evidence requirement below is aimed at **unsearched absence** and **obsolescence**, not at fabrication. Any reviewer who reads them as anti-hallucination machinery has the wrong model of the failure.\\n\\n### The table's actual defect: coverage\\n\\n12 rows. `research/` has **37 entry categories**. The single largest, `research/developer-tools/` (71 entries), maps to no row. Also unmapped: `ai-design-tools` (12), `data-infrastructure` (10), `ai-research-tools` (9), `api-frameworks` (8), `llm-infrastructure` (7), `prompt-engineering` (7), `ml-infrastructure` (6), `async-libraries` (4), `ai-writing-tools` (4), `documentation-tools` (3), `ai-observability` (3), plus ~14 singleton categories. Roughly half the corpus sits outside every row. An agent handed twelve rows stops at twelve rows. Not fixable by editing the table.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 5 What Gets Deleted\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Delete | Where | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `.claude/agents/research-utilization-assessor.md` (whole agent, 164 lines) | \u2014 | Cannot file backlog items (`:156`); forbidden from reading local files on its early-exit path (`:163-164`); discovery node is unguided prose (`:35`); its 48 outputs have no reader. Its *question* survives as the `depend` match verb. |\\n| `## Mapping Patterns to Local Systems` | `research-insight-extractor.md:49-75` | 12 domains vs 37 categories; anchors the agent to its own rows. Replaced by \u00a72's procedure. Justified by `AGENTS.md:310-312`. **Not deleted for staleness \u2014 it resolves.** |\\n| `Empty{}` gate + `Skip` node | `research-insight-extractor.md:30-31` | Makes the entry's own section terminal. |\\n| `If the entry has no Relevance or Patterns section\u2026 stop` | `research-insight-extractor.md:259` | Same rule, second copy. |\\n| Utilization spawn + relay | `SKILL.md:106-108`, `:119`, `:153`, `:193`, `:198`, `:434` | Dead with the agent. Cuts per-entry fan-out from 3 to 2. |\\n| `research/insights/*-utilization.md` (48) and `research/utilization/*.md` (2) | \u2014 | **Only after \u00a76's baseline is measured, in a separate commit.** Per `AGENTS.md` \\\"Deletion safety\\\": no replacement carries their content forward, so this is a deliberate drop of superseded analysis, not a migration. Flag it to the owner as such before executing. |\\n\\n**Not deleted: the Relevance section.** Required at `validate_research.py:54`, and #3530 has just given it a shape that earns its keep as the extractor's seed. Deleting it is a 331-file migration that changes no outcome.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## 8 Execution Order\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSequenced against what is in flight. Nothing here touches `entry-template.md`, `extraction-methodology.md`, or `.claude/agents/research-curator.md` \u2014 those belong to the entry-production agent.\\n\\n1. **Wait for `research-curator/relevance-anchors` (#3530) to land.** Phase 1c is this design's seed; editing the extractor against a moving Relevance shape is rework.\\n2. **Wait for `research-curator/cull` (#3529) to land.** It touches `SKILL.md`, so \u00a75's `SKILL.md` deletions conflict textually otherwise. It deletes `.claude/agents/research-context-agent.md` \u2014 whose Phase 2 \\\"Search & Match\\\" is the closest existing relative of what \u00a72 specifies. **Read it before writing \u00a72's procedure and take its dimension table's shape, not its terminal action:** it appended prose to the research entry, which is why nothing ever consumed it. This design's terminal action is a backlog item, which has a consumer.\\n3. **Baseline measurement** (\u00a76 metrics 1 and 3 over the existing 123 + 48 analysis files). Before any deletion.\\n4. **Edit `research-insight-extractor.md`:** delete `:30-31`, `:49-75`, `:259`; rewrite the flowchart as Phase A / Phase B; add the `borrow`/`depend` verbs, the promotion rule, the cap-5, \u00a72a's provenance rule, \u00a73a's evidence format; set `model: sonnet` in frontmatter (currently `opus` at `:4`). Keep #3530's `**Absence evidence**` field \u2014 it becomes the filing gate's absence slot rather than an advisory \u2014 and fix `:153` to carry quoted text per \u00a73a.\\n5. **Delete `research-utilization-assessor.md`; strip its spawn and relay from `SKILL.md`** (`:106-108`, `:119`, `:153`, `:193`, `:198`, `:434`).\\n6. **Add the Gate 4 retraction line** to `SKILL.md`'s Entry Review section (`:300-306`) \u2014 **after or with #3534.**\\n7. **A/B on 20 entries**; report metrics 1-4.\\n8. **Delete the 50 utilization files**, separate commit, only after step 7.\\n\\nSteps 4-6 are one PR. Steps 3, 7, 8 are separate. The filing gate itself is #3535's work, not this item's.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related Items\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **#3535** \u2014 the filing gate. **Same behaviour as this plan's \u00a73b; #3535 is the specification and this item defers to it.**\\n- **#3534** \u2014 Gate 4 cannot express path drift, so it attributes repo refactors to research agents. Must land before or with \u00a78 step 6's retraction line.\\n- **#3536** \u2014 Rule 2a enforced retroactively.\\n- PR #3529 (`research-curator/cull`) and PR #3530 (`research-curator/relevance-anchors`) \u2014 both must land first, per \u00a78.\\n- PRs #3527 and #3528 are being closed unmerged. **Do not build on either.** #3527's marker gating is referenced in \u00a72a only as the measured evidence for the 86% constraint, not as a design to adopt.\\n</div>\\n\\n## 6 How Anyone Would Know It Worked\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun the new extractor over the **20 entries that already have `-improvements.md` files** and compare, per entry:\\n\\n**Metric 1 \u2014 discovery independence. The one that distinguishes the two processes.**\\n> Share of proposals naming a local path that the entry's own Relevance section does not name.\\n\\nThe current extractor cannot exceed its Relevance section by construction (`:30-33`), so its baseline is near zero by design, and the measurement confirms or refutes that reading. The new process should be high. This is the single number separating \\\"found an overlap\\\" from \\\"inherited a claim\\\".\\n\\n**Metric 2 \u2014 category reach.**\\n> Proposals per entry, grouped by `research/` category, old vs new.\\n\\nSpecific and falsifiable: the gain should concentrate in the ~158 entries in categories with no table row (`developer-tools`, `ai-design-tools`, `data-infrastructure`, \u2026) and be near zero in the eight categories the table already covered. **If the gain is flat across categories, the table was not the constraint and this plan misdiagnosed the problem.**\\n\\n**Metric 3 \u2014 unsearched absence.**\\n> For every proposal claiming \\\"no local system does X\\\": does the recorded search command, re-run today, still return zero?\\n\\nThe cocoindex item is a metric-3 failure \u2014 `research/insights/2026-03-10-cocoindex-code-improvements.md:37` asserts no semantic code search exists while `plugins/python3-development/skills/semantic-code-search/SKILL.md` and `plugins/python-engineering/agents/semantic-code-search.md` both do. One `grep -ril \\\"semantic\\\" plugins/` would have caught it. Automatable; should be 100% and stay 100%.\\n\\n**Metric 4 \u2014 yield per dollar.**\\n> Items filed per entry x cost per entry, and \u2014 30 days on \u2014 how many of those issues are open, closed-as-done, or closed-as-wrong.\\n\\nThe only metric testing the owner's actual constraint. Metrics 1-3 can all improve while metric 4 says the step should run on a subset.\\n\\n**Baseline to capture before deleting anything:** metrics 1 and 3 over the existing 123 improvements files and 48 utilization files. That is the only remaining reason those 48 files exist.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"a185287938dc3ebdfaf778a82e870175b70d6f7dc4d07837b650568d0d579785\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUPuzIw\"}"}